<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=eradication-of-smallpox">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eradication of smallpox</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Smallpox eradication</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The global eradication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> was certified by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-health-organization">World Health Organization</a> in May 1980, after vaccination campaigns throughout the 19th and 20th centuries. The disease had killed an estimated 400,000 Europeans each year in the 18th century and, in the 20th century, an estimated 250 to 500 million people. In 1967 the WHO estimated that 15 million people contracted it and two million died. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-jenner">Edward Jenner</a>'s discovery in 1796 that cowpox inoculated against smallpox followed centuries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/variolation">variolation</a>, first practised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the 10th century. Smallpox is one of two infectious diseases eradicated, the other being rinderpest, declared eradicated in 2011.</p></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>May 1980</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Worldwide, certified by the World Health Organization</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Another passage states that smallpox is thought to have been eradicated since 1979.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox-vaccine">Vaccination<span>1796</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=eradication-of-smallpox">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
